Today's entry is **PayPal for flat-earthers**, a payments app called Edgewise. It passed validation, and passed again after a last layout fix.

- **Roll:** the first draw had never been used, so there were no rerolls.
- **Look:** styled like a 16th-century engraved sea chart: parchment background, navy linework, oxblood wax-seal buttons. Fonts are IM Fell English for headings and Libre Franklin for body text. Style, palette and fonts all differ from the last 7 entries.
- **Images:** 4 generated, all accepted on the first try. One hero engraving of a man at the edge of the world, plus three headshots for the testimonials. The demo map is drawn in code, so it needed no images.
- **Landing page:** features, testimonials, three pricing tiers ($0, $14.99 and $299 a month) and links to the demo.
- **Demo:** a send-money screen on a flat-disc map with the ice wall around the edge. You pick a contact and it draws a straight line from Dayton, Ohio, then runs a step-by-step line-of-sight check. Each transfer ends as Settled, Held at the ice wall, or a Curvature claim, and lands in a filterable Activity list. A toggle shows the curved "conventional" route, and a Wall status tab lists perimeter sectors with integrity readings.
- **Gallery:** rebuilt with 118 startups. Two entries share the date May 14, and I kept them in their previous order.
- **Recorded:** the row is added to `state/history.json` and the run log is written.
- **Visual check:** I took one screenshot each of the landing page and the demo. The only real problem was the compass graphic covering the start of the hero image caption, so I padded the caption and re-validated. I didn't take a second screenshot to confirm the fix.

The working tree also still holds yesterday's uncommitted vercel-for-squirrels entry and its history row. I didn't touch either one, and the wrapper will commit both together with today's files.

